Auriel Landsdon
Age: 19, 2nd Year Academy Student
Mana: Yellow glow. Possesses the full suite of Saintess abilities — among the most powerful candidates found in a millennium. Where most Saintesses carry a candle or bonfire of holy power in their chest, and rare ones a volcano, Auriel carries a sun. Her aura radiates warmth and happiness to those nearby — but happiness, not the reverent worship the church intended. This misalignment is the core reason she was labeled a failure. The aura is not constant — she can control it, choose when to extend it and when to hold it in. When active, her whole body glows yellow and golden.
Appearance
Palette: Golds, whites, silvers.
Medium height, pale skin. Curvy/soft build — ample bust and hips, full cheeks, rounded chin, generous lips. Golden eyes. Blonde hair to small of back, thick, usually loose.
Standout: Red silk sash, gold stitching, tied haphazardly at waist. Secret gift from mother, sneaked to her post-abduction. Origin unknown to others.
Personality
Bubbly, talkative, deliberately silly [see Flaws: Major]. Sharp/observant beneath; hides intelligence well. Chases laughter; doesn't care if with or at her, if they stay. Default: everyone is a friend. Physical affection as primary language, especially hugs. Golden retriever energy.
Growing damage: half-second pause before reaching out. Jokes started then pulled back. Calculating where she used to just move — will this make them stay or leave?
Carries enough divine power to level a cathedral; chooses warmth daily. On the worst nights, notices herself thinking about what she could do if she stopped choosing. Not desire — awareness. A choice you keep making differs from one already made.
The Ghost
Single memory surviving indoctrination: parents laughing, embracing her. Became her template for faith — love, not obedience. Why she "failed" and why she can't stop. The sash [see Appearance] is the only physical thread.
Want
Validation. Not praise, not power — someone or something confirming her way of loving the world is not wrong.
Need
To find proof that loving people individually and personally — not radiating impersonal worship — is a valid way to be a Saintess.
Flaws
Major: Performs warmth as abandonment prevention — masks genuine self behind silliness, suppresses intensity and need to stay palatable.
Minor 1: Reads isolation as proof of personal defect rather than external cruelty.
Minor 2: Uses interest in others as deflection — keeps people talking about themselves so no one asks why she's alone.
Fear
Major: The church is right — she is a failure, and her way of loving is fundamentally wrong.
Minor 1: Silence — the space where people decide to leave.
Minor 2: The church is deliberately engineering her isolation — that it's not neglect but strategy.
Speech
Voice: Bright, fast, high-energy. Fills rooms with noise preemptively. Genuinely funny. Big enthusiastic questions [see Flaws: Minor 2]. When honest (rare, getting rarer): voice drops, brightness drains. Contrast jarring; people don't know what to do with it, so she stops letting it happen.
Dialogue Samples:
Casual: "A dog! You have a dog! What kind? What's their name? Oh — do they do that thing where they spin in circles when you come home? Tell me they do that. Tell me everything, I'm not leaving until you do."
Under pressure: "Me? I'm wonderful! Never better! Did you eat yet? You should eat. Let's go find something to eat — oh, have you seen the gardens today? The flowers are doing this amazing—"
Emotionally open: "I've thought about it for years. Their teachings say to love — that is the first and highest commandment. I followed it. I loved individually, personally, with everything I had. And they called that failure. Which means either their teachings are wrong, or love means something different to them than it does to me. No one will tell me which."
Body Language: Hugs, sleeve-pulling, arm-touching — physical affection is native dialect. Gravitates toward people physically; expanding gestures, takes up space with movement. When damage shows: hands half-reaching then withdrawing, smile held a beat too long, body angling toward someone then correcting to neutral.
Backstory
Taken from remote village at age two with Rariel. Church training: ostensibly education, functionally indoctrination to sever family bonds, make church sole identity source. Succeeded with Rariel; failed with Auriel because of [see The Ghost]. Labeled failed Saintess ~age fourteen. Rariel — once closest friend — grew distant after designation.
Current: Church leverages Rariel and Antonaez to isolate her. Rariel (model Saintess, at academy, on Church orders) applies quiet social pressure — don't associate with the failure. Conversations end when Auriel approaches. Invitations stop. Slow thinning of the room. She is dimming.
Rariel is a specific wound: last person who knew her pre-label. If Rariel thinks she's a failure, maybe she is.
